WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.440 --> 00:00:06.710
SIERRA: They say you can’t pick your family
or your coworkers.

00:00:06.710 --> 00:00:09.530
The same goes for global relations.

00:00:09.530 --> 00:00:13.920
To quote a tweet once posted by my boss Richard
Haass “You have to deal with the leaders

00:00:13.920 --> 00:00:16.299
that exist, not the ones you prefer.”

00:00:16.299 --> 00:00:22.840
The United States likes to think of itself
as upholding values - including those of democracy,

00:00:22.840 --> 00:00:25.220
freedom, and human rights.

00:00:25.220 --> 00:00:29.550
But maintaining global partnerships often
means dealing with those you may not choose

00:00:29.550 --> 00:00:31.240
to call your allies.

00:00:31.240 --> 00:00:35.790
Administrations often have to balance values
with interests, which can involve troubling

00:00:35.790 --> 00:00:37.270
compromises.

00:00:37.270 --> 00:00:40.829
Such is the case in our relationship with
Saudi Arabia.

00:00:40.829 --> 00:00:45.550
For starters, they have a lot of oil, which
helps them to dominate the global market and

00:00:45.550 --> 00:00:48.520
control what we pay at the pump.

00:00:48.520 --> 00:00:49.820
And it gets a lot more complicated.

00:00:49.820 --> 00:00:53.350
I’m Gabrielle Sierra and this is Why It
Matters.

00:00:53.350 --> 00:00:58.840
Today, the US-Saudi relationship, what is
it built on, what it looks like, and what

00:00:58.840 --> 00:01:02.559
is happening within the country that makes
this partnership controversial?

00:01:02.559 --> 00:01:09.350
Gabrielle SIERRA: So what is Saudi Arabia's
role in the world of energy?

00:01:09.350 --> 00:01:13.390
Steven COOK: I think the word that first comes
to mind when someone says Saudi Arabia is

00:01:13.390 --> 00:01:20.729
oil and that's because Saudi Arabia is among,
if not the most important, oil producer in

00:01:20.729 --> 00:01:22.060
the world.

00:01:22.060 --> 00:01:24.020
SIERRA: This is Steven Cook.

00:01:24.020 --> 00:01:29.380
He is a senior fellow for Middle East and
Africa studies here at the Council on Foreign

00:01:29.380 --> 00:01:30.380
Relations.

00:01:30.380 --> 00:01:35.659
COOK: It can produce a lot of oil at a relatively
low cost, and it can do that relatively quickly.

00:01:35.659 --> 00:01:40.190
SIERRA: How much of our energy supply in the
US comes from Saudi oil?

00:01:40.190 --> 00:01:42.590
COOK: It's actually not as much as many people
think.

00:01:42.590 --> 00:01:48.790
And the fact of the matter is that in terms
of crude oil imports, Saudi Arabia supply

00:01:48.790 --> 00:01:51.119
6% of the American supply.

00:01:51.119 --> 00:01:55.460
SIERRA: Wait, hold the phone, just 6 percent?

00:01:55.460 --> 00:01:59.420
COOK: About 500,000 barrels of oil for the
United States on a daily basis.

00:01:59.420 --> 00:02:02.500
This trails well behind Canada, Mexico.

00:02:02.500 --> 00:02:03.549
SIERRA: Oh.

00:02:03.549 --> 00:02:07.590
COOK: Canada's by far the biggest supplier
of oil to the United States.

00:02:07.590 --> 00:02:11.819
Of course the United States is the world's
leading producer of oil.

00:02:11.819 --> 00:02:15.020
The United States produces somewhere in the
neighborhood of 18 and a half million barrels

00:02:15.020 --> 00:02:20.019
a day, but it also consumes 21 and a half
million barrels a day.

00:02:20.019 --> 00:02:26.239
That's why it is so important in thinking
about a global oil market.

00:02:26.239 --> 00:02:27.239
What are markets made of?

00:02:27.239 --> 00:02:32.280
At the very basics, your economics 101, supply
and demand.

00:02:32.280 --> 00:02:38.310
So when the market is out of balance, Saudi
Arabia is also often looked upon as what energy

00:02:38.310 --> 00:02:42.780
analysts and others call the swing producer,
a country that can ramp up production relatively

00:02:42.780 --> 00:02:48.030
quickly at a relatively inexpensive manner
to bring the oil market back into balance.

00:02:48.030 --> 00:02:53.670
So, this gives you a sense that even though
Saudi Arabia is not hugely important to the

00:02:53.670 --> 00:02:58.811
domestic oil market, because it's a global
market and as I said, because Saudi Arabia

00:02:58.811 --> 00:03:04.379
is kind of the low cost producer of choice
and can kind of metaphorically turn the spigot

00:03:04.379 --> 00:03:07.330
on and off, that's what makes it very, very
important.

00:03:07.330 --> 00:03:08.330
SIERRA: Got it.

00:03:08.330 --> 00:03:09.330
Right.

00:03:09.330 --> 00:03:13.670
So it's less that we need their oil and more
that they're a huge influence in the global

00:03:13.670 --> 00:03:15.580
oil market, and we depend on that.

00:03:15.580 --> 00:03:16.670
COOK: Right.

00:03:16.670 --> 00:03:21.980
SIERRA: Simply put, when Saudi Arabia decides
to release more oil to the market, gas prices

00:03:21.980 --> 00:03:23.050
go down.

00:03:23.050 --> 00:03:26.319
And that’s good for anyone who has to fill
up their car.

00:03:26.319 --> 00:03:31.360
High gas prices are a lethal political issue
in the US, and for decades, they have been

00:03:31.360 --> 00:03:34.840
a strong and predictable force in approval
polls.

00:03:34.840 --> 00:03:39.840
This makes Saudi Arabia an important consideration
for U.S. policymakers.

00:03:39.840 --> 00:03:45.220
SIERRA: How did this country become the world's
biggest oil powerhouse?

00:03:45.220 --> 00:03:48.410
COOK: Well, it's easy enough to say.

00:03:48.410 --> 00:03:55.510
Look, in 1933, standard oil of California
got a concession to prospect for oil in Saudi

00:03:55.510 --> 00:03:56.510
Arabia.

00:03:56.510 --> 00:04:04.030
In March, 1938, the Demam seven, well blew
and Saudi Arabia had a commercial amount of

00:04:04.030 --> 00:04:05.900
oil as a result.

00:04:05.900 --> 00:04:06.900
That's how it became.

00:04:06.900 --> 00:04:09.909
Then it was just a year afterwards, a little
bit of year afterwards that the first commercial

00:04:09.909 --> 00:04:12.720
exports of Saudi oil went on the market.

00:04:12.720 --> 00:04:17.590
The king flipped the switch, and it filled
a tanker at Ras Tanura which is one of the

00:04:17.590 --> 00:04:21.440
most important oil terminals in the Persian
Gulf and off it went.

00:04:21.440 --> 00:04:25.870
And since then, they discovered the world's
largest single oil field, or believed to be

00:04:25.870 --> 00:04:29.979
in the Eastern province of Saudi Arabia, the
Al-Ghawar Field.

00:04:29.979 --> 00:04:32.000
That's really how it became this powerhouse.

00:04:32.000 --> 00:04:37.560
It's been important to Western economic development
ever since.

00:04:37.560 --> 00:04:41.300
SIERRA: But the relationship has other important
features.

00:04:41.300 --> 00:04:46.710
While Saudi Arabia is providing oil, the United
States is providing weapons.

00:04:46.710 --> 00:04:48.289
A lot of them.

00:04:48.289 --> 00:04:56.030
From 2015-2019, around 74% of Saudi Arabia's
arms imports were from the United States.

00:04:56.030 --> 00:05:01.040
In the fall of last year, the U.S. State Department
approved an arms sale to Saudi Arabia worth

00:05:01.040 --> 00:05:02.040
$650 million.

00:05:02.040 --> 00:05:09.760
And just this summer the US sold patriot missiles
to Saudi Arabia in a deal valued at over $3

00:05:09.760 --> 00:05:10.760
billion.

00:05:10.760 --> 00:05:13.070
COOK: It's big business.

00:05:13.070 --> 00:05:18.800
People think in terms of Saudi Arabia and
oil, but also think in terms of Saudi Arabia

00:05:18.800 --> 00:05:23.680
and Americans weapons manufacturers, that
employ lots of people in the Fort Worth area

00:05:23.680 --> 00:05:30.910
or in South Carolina, in other places where,
California, where they manufacture particularly

00:05:30.910 --> 00:05:38.460
airframes, the F16, the F15, precision guided
munitions, The Saudis are big purchasers of

00:05:38.460 --> 00:05:39.669
those things.

00:05:39.669 --> 00:05:43.910
SIERRA: But there are further significant
aspects to US-Saudi ties.

00:05:43.910 --> 00:05:48.241
Saudi Arabia, historically, has also acted
as an important geopolitical ally.

00:05:48.241 --> 00:05:56.490
Madawi AL-RASHEED: It's extremely important
to emphasize that the relationship started

00:05:56.490 --> 00:06:05.130
in 1945, but it evolved over the last 70,
80 years and few other issues became important.

00:06:05.130 --> 00:06:07.389
SIERRA: This is Madawi Al-Rasheed.

00:06:07.389 --> 00:06:12.510
She is a visiting professor at the Middle
East Center at the London School of Economics.

00:06:12.510 --> 00:06:19.090
AL-RASHEED: So for example, in the 1980s,
Saudi Arabia became extremely important for

00:06:19.090 --> 00:06:22.949
the US, especially after the Iranian revolution
in 1979.

00:06:22.949 --> 00:06:29.910
So the US was searching for another country
in the region in order to build its military

00:06:29.910 --> 00:06:36.190
capabilities as a counter country to Iran,
especially after Iran adopted very hostile

00:06:36.190 --> 00:06:38.759
rhetoric against the US.

00:06:38.759 --> 00:06:45.849
And therefore, Saudi Arabia became drawn further
into the American regional hegemony, military

00:06:45.849 --> 00:06:48.770
bases, armament, et cetera.

00:06:48.770 --> 00:06:51.610
SIERRA: And it goes beyond this as well.

00:06:51.610 --> 00:06:56.580
Experts say that it has also been in the U.S’s
interests to improve the Saudi relationship

00:06:56.580 --> 00:06:59.400
with staunch US ally, Israel.

00:06:59.400 --> 00:07:03.979
COOK: The Israeli Saudi relationship is, it's
an important reason this is something that

00:07:03.979 --> 00:07:07.410
the United States has been driving for many,
many years.

00:07:07.410 --> 00:07:10.629
It's actually not a function of any US effort.

00:07:10.629 --> 00:07:14.300
Or maybe it's a function effect of what the
US is doing.

00:07:14.300 --> 00:07:20.800
But that Israel and Saudi Arabia share a concern
over the exercise of Iranian power around

00:07:20.800 --> 00:07:21.800
the region.

00:07:21.800 --> 00:07:24.530
It's not just Iran's nuclear activities.

00:07:24.530 --> 00:07:31.160
It's its proxies around the region, and the
fact that it funds Hezbollah, Houthis in Yemen

00:07:31.160 --> 00:07:36.690
has supported, Bashar Al Assad in Syria lends
its support to Palestinian Islamic Jihad,

00:07:36.690 --> 00:07:37.850
as well as hammas.

00:07:37.850 --> 00:07:46.169
And so like in the the way in which Iran has
in part driven relations between Israel and

00:07:46.169 --> 00:07:53.750
the UAE and Israel and Bahrain, it is also
playing a role in the far less open, but nevertheless,

00:07:53.750 --> 00:07:57.680
clearly there relationship between Saudi Arabia
and Israel.

00:07:57.680 --> 00:07:59.970
Now, also part of this is the fact that the
United States it neither is neither Israeli,

00:07:59.970 --> 00:08:04.180
the Israeli government nor the Saudi government
agree with the American approach to Iran.

00:08:04.180 --> 00:08:08.639
So, but nevertheless, the fact that the Saudis
and Israelis seem to have these under the

00:08:08.639 --> 00:08:14.639
table relations, the US wants to encourage
them, because expanding what's called the

00:08:14.639 --> 00:08:17.330
circle of peace is very, very important.

00:08:17.330 --> 00:08:20.330
I think people believe that that would be
easier.

00:08:20.330 --> 00:08:23.889
With good US Saudi relations, then the Saudis
and the United States, the Saudis and Americans

00:08:23.889 --> 00:08:24.950
at odds with each other.

00:08:24.950 --> 00:08:25.950
So it's that in addition to oil, obviously,
being, you know, first, among all other reasons

00:08:25.950 --> 00:08:26.950
that the United States and Saudi the United
States wants to have good relations with Saudi

00:08:26.950 --> 00:08:27.950
Arabia.

00:08:27.950 --> 00:08:29.909
SIERRA: So there are multiple reasons that
each country needs each other.

00:08:29.909 --> 00:08:32.940
And that motivates us both to maintain this
partnership.

00:08:32.940 --> 00:08:37.830
COOK: Traditionally, it’s been, we have
provided security.

00:08:37.830 --> 00:08:43.279
It's not written down anywhere, but we have
ensured the sea lanes and provide security

00:08:43.279 --> 00:08:48.700
to the oil fields of the Gulf, and the Saudis
have worked to maintain a stable oil market.

00:08:48.700 --> 00:08:50.380
So, you know, do we have leverage?

00:08:50.380 --> 00:08:51.420
Do they have leverage?

00:08:51.420 --> 00:08:52.600
Are we more important to them?

00:08:52.600 --> 00:08:53.770
Are they more important to us?

00:08:53.770 --> 00:08:54.770
SIERRA: Right?

00:08:54.770 --> 00:08:56.340
I was going to say, who has the upper hand
here?

00:08:56.340 --> 00:08:58.310
COOK: There's this codependency here.

00:08:58.310 --> 00:08:59.310
SIERRA: Mm-hmm.

00:08:59.310 --> 00:09:04.100
SIERRA: This unwritten contract between the
two countries is mutually beneficial.

00:09:04.100 --> 00:09:09.519
The US offers a security umbrella of sorts
and secure transport, and Saudi Arabia in

00:09:09.519 --> 00:09:11.910
return, maintains stability in oil markets.

00:09:11.910 --> 00:09:16.310
It is a relationship based on two different
needs that must be met.

00:09:16.310 --> 00:09:18.339
That’s sort of a simple set up.

00:09:18.339 --> 00:09:23.730
But the problems come when you examine the
relationship with a wider lens and look at

00:09:23.730 --> 00:09:28.470
some of the other actions Saudi Arabia has
taken over the years, and is still taking.

00:09:28.470 --> 00:09:33.360
CGTN: The latest statement regarding what
happened to Jamal Kashoggi came from Istanbul’s

00:09:33.360 --> 00:09:37.959
chief of prosecutor’s office, according
to this statement as soon as he entered the

00:09:37.959 --> 00:09:42.861
Saudi consulate on October 2nd, was choked
to death and his body was dismembered and

00:09:42.861 --> 00:09:43.861
disposed of…

00:09:43.861 --> 00:09:49.769
CBC News: Today yet another airstrike killing
civilians was blamed on the Saudi led coalition

00:09:49.769 --> 00:09:52.290
fighting Houthi rebels backed by Iran.

00:09:52.290 --> 00:09:56.510
NBC News: So what we’re seeing in Saudi
Arabia at the moment is a brutal crackdown

00:09:56.510 --> 00:10:00.100
on anyone who dares to criticize the government…

00:10:00.100 --> 00:10:01.100
Peaceful activists, women’s rights activists,
political writers, bloggers…

00:10:01.100 --> 00:10:02.100
they’ve been persecuted.

00:10:02.100 --> 00:10:05.730
SIERRA: Saudi Arabia has done a lot of things
that violate international norms that the

00:10:05.730 --> 00:10:11.490
US and other nations have signed on to uphold:
criminalization of dissent, execution for

00:10:11.490 --> 00:10:17.260
nonviolent offenses, restrictions on free
expression, and airstrikes in Yemen that have

00:10:17.260 --> 00:10:18.920
led to civilian casualties.

00:10:18.920 --> 00:10:23.959
It’s no surprise then that over the years,
critics of American foreign policy have often

00:10:23.959 --> 00:10:31.170
cited the Saudi relationship as problematic
and even a prime example of American hypocrisy.

00:10:31.170 --> 00:10:37.940
SIERRA: So in recent years, what have been
the major points of disagreement or conflict

00:10:37.940 --> 00:10:39.829
between the US and Saudi Arabia?

00:10:39.829 --> 00:10:47.860
AL-RASHEED: In the US, there is a current
that questions the integrity of this relationship

00:10:47.860 --> 00:10:54.610
between a country that claims to lead the
democratic world and a dictatorship like Saudi

00:10:54.610 --> 00:10:55.610
Arabia.

00:10:55.610 --> 00:11:02.220
Well, obviously, those people who know about
foreign relations, the US has maintained very

00:11:02.220 --> 00:11:08.980
close contact with nasty dictators in South
America, in Latin America, in the Middle East.

00:11:08.980 --> 00:11:13.750
SIERRA: As Madawi points out, our relationship
with Saudi Arabia is not unique.

00:11:13.750 --> 00:11:17.940
We often have relationships with countries
and rulers that are counter to our values

00:11:17.940 --> 00:11:20.089
in order to meet another need.

00:11:20.089 --> 00:11:24.060
Critics have also questioned our relationship
with China for human rights violations and

00:11:24.060 --> 00:11:26.290
Pakistan because of their shifting loyalties.

00:11:26.290 --> 00:11:33.410
AL-RASHEED: And there is this current that
doesn't accept the fact that so-called democracy

00:11:33.410 --> 00:11:41.690
like the US could have this intimate relationship
based on interests purely.

00:11:41.690 --> 00:11:45.100
And this current got very stronger after 9/11.

00:11:45.100 --> 00:11:53.680
Let's not forget that 15 of the hijackers
who hit the Twin Towers in New York were Saudi.

00:11:53.680 --> 00:11:57.370
And therefore, many people in the US questioned
this relationship.

00:11:57.370 --> 00:12:03.019
CBS News: Newly declassified documents show
links between the 9/11 hijackers and Saudi

00:12:03.019 --> 00:12:05.400
nationals with connections to the Saudi government

00:12:05.400 --> 00:12:11.550
CBS News: It was not possible for 19 men to
kill nearly 3,000 Americans that day without

00:12:11.550 --> 00:12:15.889
a support network and a support structure
that helped them move seamlessly around the

00:12:15.889 --> 00:12:19.690
United States for a nearly two year period.

00:12:19.690 --> 00:12:28.350
AL-RASHEED: Saudi Arabia was also the homeland
of a very radical Wahhabi Islamic tradition

00:12:28.350 --> 00:12:30.720
that inspired global jihad.

00:12:30.720 --> 00:12:36.480
And therefore, people in the US always had
question mark about how close the US could

00:12:36.480 --> 00:12:42.470
get to Saudi Arabia without jeopardizing its
own national interests.

00:12:42.470 --> 00:12:48.800
COOK: What you're getting at is this question
that has bedeviled American policy makers

00:12:48.800 --> 00:12:53.160
for a very, very long time, which is the United
States is supposed to stand for something

00:12:53.160 --> 00:12:58.970
in the world, that American values are important
to Americans in the conduct of our politics

00:12:58.970 --> 00:13:01.149
at home, but also abroad.

00:13:01.149 --> 00:13:05.029
We don't always live up to those ideals and
values, but we like to think that we strive

00:13:05.029 --> 00:13:06.410
towards them.

00:13:06.410 --> 00:13:13.910
In the Middle East, we have never really done
that because our friends in the region don't

00:13:13.910 --> 00:13:17.070
share our values and our ideals.

00:13:17.070 --> 00:13:24.889
Yet, they are the people who sit a top lots
and lots of oil, which is important to the

00:13:24.889 --> 00:13:28.700
functioning of the American economy and in
fact the global economy.

00:13:28.700 --> 00:13:33.070
So we've been willing, and of course it makes
people uncomfortable, but we've been willing

00:13:33.070 --> 00:13:41.430
to shunt aside those questions of human rights
and decency and tolerance because the Saudis

00:13:41.430 --> 00:13:45.480
are among oil producers the most important,
arguably.

00:13:45.480 --> 00:13:51.110
SIERRA: Okay, guess it’s time to take a
deeper dive into the inner workings of the

00:13:51.110 --> 00:13:53.740
country and what life is like.

00:13:53.740 --> 00:13:56.700
Just what sort of partner are we partnering
with?

00:13:56.700 --> 00:13:59.889
SIERRA: So let's look inside Saudi Arabia.

00:13:59.889 --> 00:14:01.670
So can you tell me some basics?

00:14:01.670 --> 00:14:03.339
Who is the current leader?

00:14:03.339 --> 00:14:10.450
COOK: The current leader is King Salman, who
is one of the remaining sons of the founder

00:14:10.450 --> 00:14:13.110
of Saudi Arabia, King Abdulaziz.

00:14:13.110 --> 00:14:16.230
He became the king and now he's the guy.

00:14:16.230 --> 00:14:17.370
But he's quite old.

00:14:17.370 --> 00:14:22.980
So the crown prince, his son Mohammed bin
Salman, is the guy who's been kind of running

00:14:22.980 --> 00:14:25.970
day to day operations for Saudi Arabia.

00:14:25.970 --> 00:14:31.740
It's almost as if King Salman is the chairman
and Mohammad bin Salman the crown prince is

00:14:31.740 --> 00:14:34.329
the president and CEO of the company.

00:14:34.329 --> 00:14:41.690
MBS was tapped to be basically the King's
wing man, right hand man, and ultimately his

00:14:41.690 --> 00:14:42.690
successor.

00:14:42.690 --> 00:14:48.910
AL-RASHEED: I think it's that intimate relationship
between father and son, and he appointed him

00:14:48.910 --> 00:14:50.510
to so many positions.

00:14:50.510 --> 00:14:53.680
So he is a Minister of Defense.

00:14:53.680 --> 00:14:55.120
He's a crown prince.

00:14:55.120 --> 00:14:58.290
He's the head of the investment fund.

00:14:58.290 --> 00:15:01.709
He basically controls Saudi Arabia today.

00:15:01.709 --> 00:15:05.839
It's a one-man show and it's Mohammed bin
Salman's show.

00:15:05.839 --> 00:15:11.960
SIERRA: And Mohammed bin Salman, aka MBS,
has made sure to secure his place as the head

00:15:11.960 --> 00:15:13.089
of the country.

00:15:13.089 --> 00:15:18.759
In 2017, a group of wealthy Saudis, royals
and senior government officials were detained

00:15:18.759 --> 00:15:24.010
by the Crown Prince in a hotel in an effort
to consolidate his power, an unprecedented

00:15:24.010 --> 00:15:25.010
move.

00:15:25.010 --> 00:15:30.590
Al Jazeera: Media reports suggest at least
20 princes, officials, and army officers have

00:15:30.590 --> 00:15:35.089
been arrested in the kingdom’s latest purge;
the royals have been accused of plotting to

00:15:35.089 --> 00:15:37.170
overthrow the crowned prince.

00:15:37.170 --> 00:15:41.200
SIERRA: But not all of MBS’s actions have
been this forceful.

00:15:41.200 --> 00:15:45.800
He has opened the country to more cultural
activities, he’s lessened the restrictive

00:15:45.800 --> 00:15:47.940
power of religious leaders.

00:15:47.940 --> 00:15:50.280
Women have been granted the ability to drive.

00:15:50.280 --> 00:15:56.519
MBS has also launched Vision 2030, an ambitious
plan intended to reduce the country’s dependence

00:15:56.519 --> 00:16:02.540
on oil, encourage massive private sector investments,
and focus on recreation and tourism.

00:16:02.540 --> 00:16:07.940
ABC News: Historic change in Saudi Arabia,
women getting behind the wheel, driving on

00:16:07.940 --> 00:16:08.940
the open road.

00:16:08.940 --> 00:16:13.880
Engadget: In January of 2021, Saudi Arabia
announced plans for its own futuristic city

00:16:13.880 --> 00:16:14.880
called, The Line.

00:16:14.880 --> 00:16:19.199
CNBC: All the players in the LIV golf invitational
set to make huge money.

00:16:19.199 --> 00:16:25.069
The tournament has already lured some of golf’s
biggest stars, Phil Mickelson, Dustin Johnson,

00:16:25.069 --> 00:16:27.130
and Sergio García.

00:16:27.130 --> 00:16:31.139
SIERRA: Is MBS popular in Saudi Arabia?

00:16:31.139 --> 00:16:35.041
How much can we know about what, let's say,
regular Saudi citizens feel about him?

00:16:35.041 --> 00:16:40.040
AL-RASHEED: In a dictatorship, you can't really
do opinion calls.

00:16:40.040 --> 00:16:46.350
And therefore, it's very difficult to assess
the popularity of Mohammed bin Salman.

00:16:46.350 --> 00:16:53.459
However, superficial observation would tell
us that, oh, when there is a concert or a

00:16:53.459 --> 00:16:58.430
popstar visiting Saudi Arabia, Saudi youth
flood to the streets.

00:16:58.430 --> 00:17:04.799
They chant, they dance and they are actually
enjoying the opening of Saudi Arabia under

00:17:04.799 --> 00:17:06.260
Mohammed bin Salman.

00:17:06.260 --> 00:17:12.110
COOK: He's been rather shrewd when it comes
to his domestic politics.

00:17:12.110 --> 00:17:20.949
And his top down authoritarian reforms have
proven to be quite popular and in many ways,

00:17:20.949 --> 00:17:21.949
revolutionary.

00:17:21.949 --> 00:17:28.530
Look, the sweet spot for him are Saudis who
are aged 18 to 50, that's the vast majority

00:17:28.530 --> 00:17:29.530
of Saudis.

00:17:29.530 --> 00:17:34.870
So the very fact that young Saudis can go
to concerts, and raves, and the movies, and

00:17:34.870 --> 00:17:40.600
young Saudi men and women who are not married
can sit at a restaurant together, and flirt,

00:17:40.600 --> 00:17:46.760
and do all things that they were never able
to do publicly before, has created what seems

00:17:46.760 --> 00:17:51.410
to be a reservoir of support for MBS.

00:17:51.410 --> 00:17:57.460
At the same time, he's built a national security
state that...

00:17:57.460 --> 00:18:01.950
Saudi Arabia is obviously never democracy,
obviously the royal family has established

00:18:01.950 --> 00:18:07.070
a means of political control, but there's
been a real increase in the surveillance of

00:18:07.070 --> 00:18:09.610
Saudi's society since MBS emerged.

00:18:09.610 --> 00:18:14.530
SIERRA: But it feels very much like, "I'm
giving you those things, no one else is giving

00:18:14.530 --> 00:18:15.530
you those things."

00:18:15.530 --> 00:18:20.090
Like I know with the women driving, it's like,
"I did this," and that's the news we hear.

00:18:20.090 --> 00:18:26.850
But we don't hear the other side where activists
were getting arrested, people who pushed for

00:18:26.850 --> 00:18:27.850
this.

00:18:27.850 --> 00:18:28.850
COOK: That's right.

00:18:28.850 --> 00:18:32.909
And so, reform can't come from below, it has
to come from MBS.

00:18:32.909 --> 00:18:39.500
And anybody who then takes his reforms, his
moves and then wants to run with it, push

00:18:39.500 --> 00:18:43.840
it further, are in very serious jeopardy.

00:18:43.840 --> 00:18:49.610
SIERRA: Many women activists in particular
have faced this jeopardy first hand, especially

00:18:49.610 --> 00:18:54.380
those who advocate against male guardianship
- it’s a system that remains in place in

00:18:54.380 --> 00:18:59.919
Saudi, and gives male family members legal
control over a female family member’s life.

00:18:59.919 --> 00:19:03.380
To hear one of these stories we reached out
to Lina Alhathloul.

00:19:03.380 --> 00:19:08.900
She is the head of monitoring and communications
at ALQST, a grassroots Saudi NGO.

00:19:08.900 --> 00:19:11.539
She told us about her sister, Loujain.

00:19:11.539 --> 00:19:17.790
CBS Evening News: …Loujain Alhathoul dared
to take the wheel in Saudi Arabia when it

00:19:17.790 --> 00:19:20.280
was banned by the conservative Islamic kingdom.

00:19:20.280 --> 00:19:23.780
Al Jazeera: After 73 days in jail, she’d
become the face of a movement.

00:19:23.780 --> 00:19:27.150
DW News: She campaigned for the right of women
to drive.

00:19:27.150 --> 00:19:36.510
Lina ALHATHOUL: So my sister Loujain is Saudi
human rights activist, more focused on women's

00:19:36.510 --> 00:19:37.510
rights.

00:19:37.510 --> 00:19:43.730
She first started when she was in Canada,
approximately in 2013.

00:19:43.730 --> 00:19:49.080
And once she finished her studies, she went
to Saudi Arabia.

00:19:49.080 --> 00:19:54.290
And the first day she landed, she actually
drove from the airport to our house, with

00:19:54.290 --> 00:20:00.470
my father filming her, saying that with his
name, that this is his daughter, that he's

00:20:00.470 --> 00:20:01.480
proud of her.

00:20:01.480 --> 00:20:02.480
And it went viral.

00:20:02.480 --> 00:20:06.490
And this is how the campaign for the women
to drive really began.

00:20:06.490 --> 00:20:08.400
And she was leading it.

00:20:08.400 --> 00:20:12.940
SIERRA: Over the years Loujain continued to
speak out for women’s rights and against

00:20:12.940 --> 00:20:14.110
male guardianship.

00:20:14.110 --> 00:20:19.210
And even though some of these rights like
women driving, did come to fruition, at times

00:20:19.210 --> 00:20:24.011
she faced harsh punishment for it - she was
once blindfolded and briefly detained, and

00:20:24.011 --> 00:20:26.150
she served time in prison.

00:20:26.150 --> 00:20:30.200
But Loujain continued her advocacy work, even
though she knew the threats to her safety

00:20:30.200 --> 00:20:31.360
were growing.

00:20:31.360 --> 00:20:35.370
ALHATHOUL: They broke into our house in 2018,
they took Loujain.

00:20:35.370 --> 00:20:38.790
They didn't tell my parents where they were
taking her.

00:20:38.790 --> 00:20:42.380
My sister disappeared for almost a month.

00:20:42.380 --> 00:20:47.110
The official media was saying that she's a
traitor, that she betrayed the nation and

00:20:47.110 --> 00:20:52.940
that she should be condemned to the maximum,
which is in Saudi, death penalty.

00:20:52.940 --> 00:20:54.270
And of course we were terrified.

00:20:54.270 --> 00:21:00.230
We had no idea what was going on, it was unprecedented,
this defamation campaign.

00:21:00.230 --> 00:21:06.550
My parents were allowed to see her three or
four months later.

00:21:06.550 --> 00:21:08.341
And it's always very difficult for me to speak
about this part.

00:21:08.341 --> 00:21:13.090
So they saw a very weakened, fragile Loujain.

00:21:13.090 --> 00:21:15.390
She looked terrified.

00:21:15.390 --> 00:21:18.390
And she refused to say anything at that time.

00:21:18.390 --> 00:21:24.000
And then came the murder of Khashoggi and
reports of torture in unofficial detention

00:21:24.000 --> 00:21:26.360
facilities came out, before Loujain talked
even.

00:21:26.360 --> 00:21:30.340
So when my parents saw her the next time,
they asked her about the torture.

00:21:30.340 --> 00:21:36.220
And that's when Loujain told them that the
months that she was held in these unofficial

00:21:36.220 --> 00:21:40.360
detention facilities, it was basically torture
facilities.

00:21:40.360 --> 00:21:50.480
And that she was electrocuted, water boarded,
flogged, beat, and sexually harassed.

00:21:50.480 --> 00:21:55.490
And she had the courage to say that one of
the men, she recognized one of the men who

00:21:55.490 --> 00:22:01.080
was Khatani, and who was MBS's right hand
man.

00:22:01.080 --> 00:22:07.880
And so after that, the trial began and the
charges explicitly mentioned her activism.

00:22:07.880 --> 00:22:12.310
And if MBS sees you as a threat, he will imprison
you and charge you with anything.

00:22:12.310 --> 00:22:17.950
So my sister, after that, she was sentenced
to five years in prison with a three year

00:22:17.950 --> 00:22:18.950
probation.

00:22:18.950 --> 00:22:19.950
So she went out earlier.

00:22:19.950 --> 00:22:22.750
And she has a five year travel ban.

00:22:22.750 --> 00:22:27.200
So I don't know how we can still believe empowerment
of women.

00:22:27.200 --> 00:22:29.150
You cannot impose change on women.

00:22:29.150 --> 00:22:36.289
You cannot silence them and tell them not
to ask for their rights and just impose anything

00:22:36.289 --> 00:22:39.600
you want and say, this is what empowerment
looks like.

00:22:39.600 --> 00:22:42.539
The process of women empowerment has to include
women.

00:22:42.539 --> 00:22:45.520
Otherwise, it's not empowerment, it's another
kind of repression.

00:22:45.520 --> 00:22:51.059
SIERRA: Lina has not seen her family since
this incident, and they are all on an unofficial

00:22:51.059 --> 00:22:52.600
travel ban.

00:22:52.600 --> 00:22:56.179
And she and her sister is not alone in her
experience.

00:22:56.179 --> 00:23:02.630
Recently, a woman named Salma al-Shehab was
sentenced to up to 34 years in prison based

00:23:02.630 --> 00:23:06.280
on posts she shared on Twitter.

00:23:06.280 --> 00:23:13.320
SIERRA: What's the cost of our close dependent
relationship with Saudi Arabia, and I mean

00:23:13.320 --> 00:23:15.299
cost in the widest sense.

00:23:15.299 --> 00:23:17.380
COOK: There's certainly a moral cost.

00:23:17.380 --> 00:23:22.650
I think that's the biggest cost is that anytime
an American president would talk about human

00:23:22.650 --> 00:23:27.279
rights and values and tolerance, especially
when it came to the Middle East, Saudi Arabia

00:23:27.279 --> 00:23:28.690
was the exception.

00:23:28.690 --> 00:23:32.990
There's always been this Saudi exception and
that's because of its role in the global oil

00:23:32.990 --> 00:23:33.990
markets.

00:23:33.990 --> 00:23:38.500
What we've seen with president Biden, who
has sought to inject American values into

00:23:38.500 --> 00:23:44.260
his foreign policy, is that he has had to
renege because of his parochial political

00:23:44.260 --> 00:23:49.770
concerns with his poll numbers, which are
affected by high gas prices, as well as the

00:23:49.770 --> 00:23:53.790
fact that there are kind of geo strategic
concerns in the Middle East.

00:23:53.790 --> 00:23:58.510
It's uncomfortable and it actually causes
some outrage among people in the Middle East

00:23:58.510 --> 00:24:03.640
who want to live in more open and democratic
societies because it's entirely obvious that

00:24:03.640 --> 00:24:09.000
despite whatever public pronouncements an
American president might make about values.Our

00:24:09.000 --> 00:24:13.690
conduct in that part of the world contradicts
those values and ideals.

00:24:13.690 --> 00:24:18.250
SIERRA: Essentially, these are the conditions
we accept when we accept a relationship with

00:24:18.250 --> 00:24:19.740
Saudi Arabia.

00:24:19.740 --> 00:24:22.030
But does it have to be this way?

00:24:22.030 --> 00:24:28.270
SIERRA: Do you think there could have been
a preferable Saudi Arabia under another potential

00:24:28.270 --> 00:24:29.270
ruler?

00:24:29.270 --> 00:24:37.149
AL-RASHEED: In my view, Saudi Arabia will
be a better country if there is serious political

00:24:37.149 --> 00:24:44.730
reform, and Saudi Arabia moves into a kind
of institutional state and a constitutional

00:24:44.730 --> 00:24:45.910
state.

00:24:45.910 --> 00:24:52.210
It's very difficult to see that a change of
prince will actually make a big difference.

00:24:52.210 --> 00:24:59.070
Saudi Arabia is an absolute monarchy with
no representation, no participation, no elected

00:24:59.070 --> 00:25:02.169
parliament, no elected government.

00:25:02.169 --> 00:25:07.210
And in my view, there is no super prince who
could actually change the situation.

00:25:07.210 --> 00:25:11.250
SIERRA: I was going to say, I mean, do you
think it's possible for Saudi to transition

00:25:11.250 --> 00:25:13.860
to a constitutional state in our lifetimes?

00:25:13.860 --> 00:25:15.970
Is there support for that in the country?

00:25:15.970 --> 00:25:19.200
AL-RASHEED: Well, that depends on several
factors.

00:25:19.200 --> 00:25:29.760
First of all, you need a society that is actually
fed up with the lack of rule of law, the long

00:25:29.760 --> 00:25:33.179
prison sentences, the lack of freedom of speech.

00:25:33.179 --> 00:25:40.860
So if there is no bottom-up pressure on the
monarchy, I don't see any change.

00:25:40.860 --> 00:25:47.179
The other thing is we need a kind of different
ruler, a different monarch who would be able

00:25:47.179 --> 00:25:48.179
to listen.

00:25:48.179 --> 00:25:51.149
But I don't see Mohammed bin Salman as listening
to anything.

00:25:51.149 --> 00:25:58.330
And third, we need an international community
ready to support the drive to democracy in

00:25:58.330 --> 00:25:59.370
Saudi Arabia.

00:25:59.370 --> 00:26:06.530
This, I don't see happening in the US again,
or the Western allies of Saudi Arabia.

00:26:06.530 --> 00:26:13.100
I don't see that they are pushing for any
kind of democratic steps, regardless of how

00:26:13.100 --> 00:26:14.470
small they are.

00:26:14.470 --> 00:26:20.789
I think simply they prefer to deal with this
close circle of princes.

00:26:20.789 --> 00:26:24.299
But I think there is a lesson to be learned.

00:26:24.299 --> 00:26:30.650
A close Saudi Arabia and undemocratic Saudi
Arabia is very dangerous, not only to Saudis,

00:26:30.650 --> 00:26:33.140
but also to the international community.

00:26:33.140 --> 00:26:41.490
Let's not forget that an absolute monarchy
under its nose, a Jihadi trend flourished,

00:26:41.490 --> 00:26:47.140
and then it became global and the damage was
actually all over the world.

00:26:47.140 --> 00:26:52.980
So a democratic Saudi Arabia would be beneficial
for not only Saudis, but also the international

00:26:52.980 --> 00:26:53.980
community.

00:26:53.980 --> 00:26:59.880
SIERRA: This is where the big question comes
- does Saudi Arabia matter so much to the

00:26:59.880 --> 00:27:03.159
United States that we are willing to continue
the partnership?

00:27:03.159 --> 00:27:06.350
SIERRA: Is this as good as it gets?

00:27:06.350 --> 00:27:09.840
Because a lot of people feel like this is
a very toxic relationship.

00:27:09.840 --> 00:27:12.070
COOK: It's a difficult and fraught relationship.

00:27:12.070 --> 00:27:17.649
It has grown more difficult in recent years
because of changes in the United States, changes

00:27:17.649 --> 00:27:21.919
in Saudi Arabia and changes in the global
political order.

00:27:21.919 --> 00:27:25.570
Does the United States want to remain as invested
in the Middle East?

00:27:25.570 --> 00:27:30.159
If that's the case, then if you look around
the region, Saudi Arabia is very important

00:27:30.159 --> 00:27:35.200
to the region and to the global economy, and
that requires the United States come to some

00:27:35.200 --> 00:27:37.240
sort of accommodation with it.

00:27:37.240 --> 00:27:42.300
Can we step back from the region yet still
get the things that we want including the

00:27:42.300 --> 00:27:44.480
free flow of energy resources?

00:27:44.480 --> 00:27:45.620
Can we pivot to Asia?

00:27:45.620 --> 00:27:50.500
These are all questions that I think we are
working out here in the United States.

00:27:50.500 --> 00:27:53.470
Some people suggest that maybe the United
States can step back from the region that

00:27:53.470 --> 00:27:55.990
these actors can actually be more responsible.

00:27:55.990 --> 00:28:00.779
As far as internally, MBS is toxic.

00:28:00.779 --> 00:28:05.850
You know, I mean, MBS, his human rights record
is not good.

00:28:05.850 --> 00:28:11.790
How do you weigh that against large numbers
of Saudis, particularly young Saudis, that

00:28:11.790 --> 00:28:19.390
sweet spot of 18 to 50 year olds who seem
to be very supportive of the reforms, not

00:28:19.390 --> 00:28:22.860
liberalization that he has undertaken from
the top down?

00:28:22.860 --> 00:28:26.960
How do you weigh that and say, "This is the
best Saudi Arabia we're going to have, or

00:28:26.960 --> 00:28:28.789
this is the worst."?

00:28:28.789 --> 00:28:34.820
In conversation with policy makers here in
Washington, if you strip away a lot of this

00:28:34.820 --> 00:28:39.779
stuff, what Mohammed bin Salman has done is
he has defanged the religious police.

00:28:39.779 --> 00:28:42.290
He has spoken out against extremism.

00:28:42.290 --> 00:28:47.880
He has allowed women to drive, a Washington
holy of holy and he's interested in normalizing

00:28:47.880 --> 00:28:50.420
relations with Israel.

00:28:50.420 --> 00:28:54.340
Those are all things that the United States
has really wanted from Saudi Arabia for a

00:28:54.340 --> 00:29:04.289
long time, but it also comes with this toxic,
this leader who is seeking to establish and

00:29:04.289 --> 00:29:09.539
consolidate his rule using very, very nasty
methods.

00:29:09.539 --> 00:29:12.190
SIERRA: So what can we do?

00:29:12.190 --> 00:29:15.590
COOK: There are three options for the relationship.

00:29:15.590 --> 00:29:21.090
The first is to allow the two countries to
essentially go their own way and not resolve

00:29:21.090 --> 00:29:22.650
underlying problems.

00:29:22.650 --> 00:29:28.039
The second is a realist rapprochement, where
we recognize the problems but the countries

00:29:28.039 --> 00:29:32.940
are too important to each other that we agreed
to move forward despite the underlying tension

00:29:32.940 --> 00:29:33.940
in the relationship.

00:29:33.940 --> 00:29:40.269
And the third is a strategic compact, where
US and Saudi officials try to deal with all

00:29:40.269 --> 00:29:44.850
of the underlying tensions, tensions between
the two governments and forge a way forward

00:29:44.850 --> 00:29:45.950
for the future.

00:29:45.950 --> 00:29:47.100
Those are three ways.

00:29:47.100 --> 00:29:49.530
Admittedly, the last one is a tall order.

00:29:49.530 --> 00:29:53.490
And because it does require both governments
to do things that they haven't necessarily,

00:29:53.490 --> 00:29:56.299
at least up until this point, haven't been
wanting to do.

00:29:56.299 --> 00:30:01.200
And so there's a sense that perhaps the best
we're going to do is that realistic approach,

00:30:01.200 --> 00:30:06.080
which which leads to this kind of very interesting
question, you know, where can you draw the

00:30:06.080 --> 00:30:07.080
line?

00:30:07.080 --> 00:30:09.372
If you look at that history of the US Saudi
relationship, we've never been, we've never

00:30:09.372 --> 00:30:10.460
been willing to draw the line.

00:30:10.460 --> 00:30:12.150
And they've never been willing to draw the
line.

00:30:12.150 --> 00:30:13.360
I mean, there were there were crises.

00:30:13.360 --> 00:30:17.549
But when it's come to, you know, Saudi human
rights violations, or its dalliance, Russia,

00:30:17.549 --> 00:30:22.840
currently, the United States government has
preferred to seek ways either to ignore those

00:30:22.840 --> 00:30:27.570
issues, or to work with with the Saudis so
that we never really actually have to draw

00:30:27.570 --> 00:30:28.570
a line.

00:30:28.570 --> 00:30:35.580
AL-RASHEED: I do not want the US to boycott
Saudi Arabia, and I don't want the US to sanction

00:30:35.580 --> 00:30:36.680
Saudi Arabia.

00:30:36.680 --> 00:30:45.399
I want the US to make its help to Saudi Arabia
conditional on Saudi Arabia abiding by international

00:30:45.399 --> 00:30:53.990
law, by international norms, and make the
US support conditional on Saudi Arabia behaving

00:30:53.990 --> 00:31:00.090
as a respectable member of the international
community rather than a pariah that could

00:31:00.090 --> 00:31:09.919
dare to send a death squad to kill journalists
abroad or spy on young Saudis in the US.

00:31:09.919 --> 00:31:17.280
And therefore, US help should actually be
linked to certain political reform in Saudi

00:31:17.280 --> 00:31:20.519
Arabia, not simply allowing women to drive.

00:31:20.519 --> 00:31:27.779
And then if there is a constituency in the
US that is concerned about this kind of dubious

00:31:27.779 --> 00:31:37.669
relationship, they should ask for more openness
when we are talking here about arms deals,

00:31:37.669 --> 00:31:43.980
when we are talking about intelligence sharing
and all those kind of things that make the

00:31:43.980 --> 00:31:46.960
relationship transparent.

00:31:46.960 --> 00:31:52.019
Obviously on the Saudi side, it's very difficult
to make it transparent because there are no

00:31:52.019 --> 00:31:55.040
accountable institutions in Saudi Arabia.

00:31:55.040 --> 00:31:57.070
As I said, it's a one man show.

00:31:57.070 --> 00:32:05.669
But in the US, we can push and Americans should
push for a more transparent relationship with

00:32:05.669 --> 00:32:11.669
Saudi Arabia simply because the US has the
institutions and the accountability.

00:32:11.669 --> 00:32:18.750
And we don't want any secret relationships
between the two countries because only under

00:32:18.750 --> 00:32:24.530
the secrecy that Mohammed bin Salman gets
away with murder.

00:32:24.530 --> 00:32:30.600
SIERRA: What would be necessary to become
independent of Saudi Arabia?

00:32:30.600 --> 00:32:37.789
COOK: If ever we could pursue a rational energy
policy where we focused on alternative energy

00:32:37.789 --> 00:32:43.649
and fuel efficiency, we would perhaps not
be independent, but not be so vulnerable and

00:32:43.649 --> 00:32:48.091
complicit with the Saudis and others, but
we haven't been willing to do it.

00:32:48.091 --> 00:32:52.909
It's just easier rather than kind of pounding
away at energy efficiency and fighting with

00:32:52.909 --> 00:32:57.600
the automobile companies and fighting with
your political opponents about it, or alternative

00:32:57.600 --> 00:33:03.580
energy in which there is, you know, tremendous
culture war issues around that issue.

00:33:03.580 --> 00:33:07.380
It's just easier to go to the Saudis and say,
Hey, you guys, can you pump some more oil

00:33:07.380 --> 00:33:08.380
here and help us out?

00:33:08.380 --> 00:33:09.380
SIERRA: Yeah.

